# Put the simulated robot at a joint configuration, without moving it there.
#
# The point of a teleport is to set up a test in one step instead of driving to the pose first, so
# this is deliberately NOT a motion: it writes the state and returns.
#
# It does NOT manage controllers, and that is the same boundary MoveIt Pro draws around its
# equivalent service. A running joint_trajectory_controller still holds the goal it was given, so
# teleporting mid-trajectory means the controller will immediately pull the arm back. Deactivate the
# controller first if that is not what you want -- doing it silently inside here would make one
# service quietly reconfigure the robot.
#
# Exactly one of joint_state / keyframe is used; keyframe wins when both are given.
moveit_msgs/RobotState joint_state
# A named keyframe defined in the MJCF model, e.g. "home". Model-defined poses beat hard-coded
# numbers in a launch file: the pose travels with the robot it belongs to.
string keyframe
# Stop the arm dead rather than leaving it with the velocity it had.
bool zero_velocity
---
bool success
string message
# Where the joints actually ended up, which is not the request when a joint was out of limits.
sensor_msgs/JointState resulting_state
